      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i keep getting an error when i try to run this macro for my monthly reports. \n\nrun-time error '1004': application-defined or object-defined error\n\nhere is the code:\n\nSub ReconcileReport()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Monthly_Recon\")\n    \n    ws.Range(\"A1\").Value = \"Date\"\n    ws.Range(\"B1\").Value = \"Amount\"\n    \n    ' this part is where it keeps crashing\n    ws.Select\n    ws.Range(\"C1:C100\").SpecialCells(xlCellTypeBlanks).Value = 0\n    \n    MsgBox \"Done!\"\nEnd Sub",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That error is a classic sign that something in your code is trying to do something the spreadsheet doesn't want to do, much like a mischievous goblin trying to steal a cookie from a very strict baker. The specific error `1004` usually means VBA is hitting a wall, often because it's trying to access a cell that doesn't exist or a range that is empty.\n\nIn your code, the line `ws.Range(\"C1:C100\").SpecialCells(xlCellTypeBlanks).Value = 0` is the culprit. It's asking Excel to find blank cells in that specific range and fill them with zeros. If there are no blank cells in C1:C100, Excel throws an error because it can't find anything to do. It's like a goblin looking for a specific shiny rock in a pile of dirt and getting frustrated when he finds nothing.\n\nTo fix this, you should check if there are actually any blank cells before trying to fill them, or simply remove the `SpecialCells` part if you just want to clear the whole range. Here is the corrected version of your macro:\n\n```vba\nSub ReconcileReport()\n    Dim",
